// 2013 GS1 (Prelims) Set A — Transcribed from "E:\UPSC question paper\2013\prilims 2013 ques & ans.docx"
// Contains question, choices (A, B, C, D), answer, explanation, and upsc review points
export const paper = { year: 2013, name: 'GS1', set: 'A' };

export const questions = [
{"no":1,"text":"What will follow if a Money Bill is substantially amended by the Rajya Sabha?","A":"The Lok Sabha may still proceed with the Bill, accepting or not accepting the recommendations of the Rajya Sabha.","B":"The Lok Sabha cannot consider the Bill further.","C":"The Lok Sabha may send the Bill to the Rajya Sabha for reconsideration.","D":"The President may call a joint sitting for passing the Bill.","ans":"A","category":"Polity & Governance","subcategory":"Parliament","tags":["Money Bill","Rajya Sabha","UPSC","2013"],"expl":"A Money Bill can be introduced only in the Lok Sabha.\nThe Rajya Sabha can only make recommendations on a Money Bill.\nThe Lok Sabha may accept or reject those recommendations.\nIf the Rajya Sabha does not return the Bill within 14 days, it is deemed passed by both Houses in the form passed by the Lok Sabha.","revisionPoints":"Money Bill → Article 110\nIntroduced only in Lok Sabha.\nRajya Sabha has only a recommendatory role.\nJoint sitting is not applicable to Money Bills.\nSpeaker of Lok Sabha certifies whether a Bill is a Money Bill."},
{"no":2,"text":"Which one of the following statements is correct?","A":"In India, the same person cannot be appointed as Governor for two or more States at the same time.","B":"High Court judges are appointed by the Governor of the State.","C":"No procedure has been laid down in the Constitution of India for the removal of a Governor.","D":"In a Union Territory having a legislative setup, the Chief Minister is appointed by the Lt. Governor on the basis of majority support.","ans":"C","category":"Polity & Governance","subcategory":"Governor","tags":["Appointment","Removal","UPSC","2013"],"expl":"The Constitution does not prescribe a specific removal procedure for the Governor.\nThe Governor holds office during the pleasure of the President.\nA person can be appointed Governor of two or more States.\nHigh Court judges are appointed by the President, not by the Governor.","revisionPoints":"Governor → Article 153 onwards\nAppointed by the President.\nHolds office during the pleasure of the President.\nNormal term → 5 years, subject to pleasure.\nSame person can serve as Governor of more than one State."},
{"no":3,"text":"Which one of the following pairs is correctly matched?\n1. Geographical Feature\n2. Region","A":"Abyssinian Plateau","B":"Atlas Mountains","C":"Guiana Highlands","D":"Okavango Basin","ans":"B","category":"Geography","subcategory":"Physical Geography","tags":["Geographical Features","World Regions","UPSC","2013"],"expl":"The Atlas Mountains are located in northwestern Africa.\nThey extend through countries including Morocco, Algeria and Tunisia.\nThe Abyssinian/Ethiopian Highlands are in eastern Africa.\nGuiana Highlands are in northern South America.\nOkavango Basin is in southern Africa.","revisionPoints":"Atlas Mountains → Northwest Africa\nAbyssinian Highlands → Ethiopia region\nGuiana Highlands → South America\nOkavango Basin → Southern Africa"},
{"no":4,"text":"With reference to the history of Indian rock-cut architecture, consider the following statements:\n1. The caves at Badami are the oldest surviving rock-cut caves in India.\n2. The Barabar rock-cut caves were originally made for Ajivikas by Emperor Chandragupta Maurya.\n3. At Ellora, caves were made for different faiths.\nWhich of the statements given above is/are correct?","A":"1 only","B":"2 and 3 only","C":"3 only","D":"1, 2 and 3","ans":"C","category":"History","subcategory":"Ancient India","tags":["Rock-cut Architecture","Badami","Barabar Caves","UPSC","2013"],"expl":"Badami caves are important examples of early Chalukyan rock-cut architecture, but they are not the oldest surviving rock-cut caves in India.\nThe Barabar caves were made during the Mauryan period for the Ajivikas, but the attribution to Chandragupta Maurya in statement 2 is incorrect.\nEllora contains Buddhist, Hindu and Jain caves, showing religious diversity.","revisionPoints":"Barabar Caves → Mauryan period\nAssociated with Ajivikas.\nEllora → Buddhist + Hindu + Jain traditions\nBadami → Chalukyan rock-cut architecture\nRock-cut architecture is an important part of ancient Indian art history."},
{"no":5,"text":"Recombinant DNA technology (Genetic Engineering) allows genes to be transferred:\n1. Across different species of plants\n2. From animals to plants\n3. From microorganisms to higher organisms","A":"1 only","B":"2 and 3 only","C":"1 and 3 only","D":"1, 2 and 3","ans":"D","category":"Science & Technology","subcategory":"Biotechnology","tags":["Recombinant DNA Technology","UPSC","2013"],"expl":"Recombinant DNA technology allows DNA sequences to be isolated, modified and transferred between organisms.\nGenes can be transferred between different plant species.\nGenes from animals or microorganisms can also be introduced into plants or other organisms.\nThis forms the basis of many genetically modified organisms (GMOs).","revisionPoints":"Recombinant DNA → combining DNA from different sources\nEnables transfer of specific genes.\nUsed in:\nGenetic engineering\nGM crops\nPharmaceutical production\nResearch\nA transferred gene can provide a specific desired trait."},
{"no":6,"text":"The Chinese traveller Yuan Chwang (Hiuen Tsang) recorded the general conditions and culture of India. Which statements are correct?\n1. Roads and river-routes were completely immune from robbery.\n2. Ordeals by fire, water and poison were used for determining innocence or guilt.\n3. Tradesmen had to pay duties at ferries and barrier stations.","A":"1 only","B":"2 and 3 only","C":"1 and 3 only","D":"1, 2 and 3","ans":"B","category":"History","subcategory":"Ancient India","tags":["Yuan Chwang","Hiuen Tsang","UPSC","2013"],"expl":"Yuan Chwang's account provides information about society, administration, religion and economy.\nStatement 1 is incorrect because roads and river routes were not completely free from robbery.\nHis account mentions ordeals as methods used in judicial practices.\nTraders paid duties at ferries and barrier stations.","revisionPoints":"Yuan Chwang/Xuanzang → Chinese Buddhist traveller\nVisited India during the reign of Harshavardhana.\nHis writings are important sources for studying 7th-century India.\nTravel accounts provide evidence about society, administration and religious institutions."},
{"no":7,"text":"Consider the following:\n1. Star tortoise\n2. Monitor lizard\n3. Pygmy hog\n4. Spider monkey\nWhich of the above are naturally found in India?","A":"1, 2 and 3 only","B":"2 and 3 only","C":"1 and 4 only","D":"1, 2, 3 and 4","ans":"A","category":"Environment & Ecology","subcategory":"Biodiversity","tags":["Star Tortoise","Pygmy Hog","UPSC","2013"],"expl":"Indian star tortoise is naturally found in India.\nMonitor lizards occur naturally in India.\nPygmy hog is a rare species found in parts of northeastern India.\nSpider monkeys are native to Central and South America, not India.","revisionPoints":"Indian star tortoise → India\nPygmy hog → Northeast India\nMonitor lizards → several parts of India.\nSpider monkey → Americas\nSpecies-distribution questions require attention to native range."},
{"no":8,"text":"Which of the following can be found as pollutants in drinking water in some parts of India?\n1. Arsenic\n2. Sorbitol\n3. Fluoride\n4. Formaldehyde\n5. Uranium","A":"1 and 3 only","B":"2, 4 and 5 only","C":"1, 3 and 5 only","D":"1, 2, 3, 4 and 5","ans":"C","category":"Environment & Ecology","subcategory":"Pollution","tags":["Drinking Water Pollutants","UPSC","2013"],"expl":"Arsenic contamination is a major groundwater concern in some regions.\nExcess fluoride can cause health problems such as fluorosis.\nUranium can occur naturally in groundwater in some areas.\nSorbitol is not identified as a major groundwater pollutant in the source.","revisionPoints":"Groundwater contamination can be:\nGeogenic → naturally occurring contaminants\nAnthropogenic → human-induced contamination\nArsenic → arsenicosis\nFluoride → fluorosis\nUranium contamination can be associated with certain geological formations."},
{"no":9,"text":"With reference to Indian History, the Members of the Constituent Assembly from the Provinces were:","A":"Directly elected by the people of those Provinces","B":"Nominated by the Indian National Congress and the Muslim League","C":"Elected by the Provincial Legislative Assemblies","D":"Selected by the Government for their expertise in constitutional matters","ans":"C","category":"Polity & Governance","subcategory":"Constituent Assembly","tags":["Constituent Assembly Members","UPSC","2013"],"expl":"Members representing the provinces were indirectly elected.\nThey were elected by the members of the Provincial Legislative Assemblies.\nThe election used proportional representation by means of the single transferable vote.\nRepresentatives of princely states were nominated by the rulers.","revisionPoints":"Constituent Assembly formed under the Cabinet Mission Plan, 1946.\nProvincial representatives → indirect election.\nPrincely State representatives → nomination by rulers.\nConstituent Assembly first met on 9 December 1946."},
{"no":10,"text":"Consider the following animals:\n1. Sea cow\n2. Sea horse\n3. Sea lion\nWhich of the above is/are mammals?","A":"1 only","B":"1 and 3 only","C":"2 and 3 only","D":"1, 2 and 3","ans":"B","category":"Environment & Ecology","subcategory":"Biodiversity","tags":["Marine Mammals","UPSC","2013"],"expl":"Sea cow refers to animals such as the manatee and dugong, which are mammals.\nSea lion is also a marine mammal.\nSea horse is a fish, despite its name.\nTherefore, only 1 and 3 are mammals.","revisionPoints":"Sea cow → mammal\nSea lion → mammal\nSea horse → fish\nMarine mammals breathe through lungs.\nNames of organisms can be misleading; classification depends on biological characteristics."},
{"no":11,"text":"Consider the following statements:\n1. An amendment to the Constitution of India can be initiated by introduction of a Bill in the Lok Sabha only.\n2. If such an amendment seeks to make changes in the federal character of the Constitution, it requires ratification by the legislatures of all the States of India.\nWhich of the statements given above is/are correct?","A":"1 only","B":"2 only","C":"Both 1 and 2","D":"Neither 1 nor 2","ans":"D","category":"Polity & Governance","subcategory":"Constitutional Amendment","tags":["Federal Character","UPSC","2013"],"expl":"A Constitutional Amendment Bill can be introduced in either House of Parliament.\nCertain amendments affecting federal provisions require ratification by at least half of the State Legislatures, not all States.\nTherefore, both statements are incorrect.","revisionPoints":"Article 368 → Constitutional amendment\nAmendment Bill can originate in Lok Sabha or Rajya Sabha.\nSome amendments require:\nSpecial majority of Parliament\nPlus ratification by at least half of State Legislatures\nThere is no joint sitting for Constitutional Amendment Bills."},
{"no":12,"text":"Attorney General of India can:\n1. Take part in the proceedings of the Lok Sabha\n2. Be a member of a committee of the Lok Sabha\n3. Speak in the Lok Sabha\n4. Vote in the Lok Sabha\nWhich of the above are correct?","A":"1 only","B":"2 and 4","C":"1, 2 and 3","D":"1 and 3 only","ans":"D","category":"Polity & Governance","subcategory":"Government Bodies","tags":["Attorney General","UPSC","2013"],"expl":"The Attorney General has the right to speak and participate in proceedings of Parliament.\nThe Attorney General does not have the right to vote.\nThe source answer also excludes membership of a Lok Sabha committee.","revisionPoints":"Attorney General → highest law officer of India\nAppointed by the President.\nCan speak and participate in Parliament.\nNo voting right merely by virtue of the office.\nConstitutional basis → Article 76."},
{"no":13,"text":"With reference to the usefulness of by-products of the sugar industry, consider the following:\n1. Bagasse can be used as biomass fuel for energy generation.\n2. Molasses can be used as feedstock for synthetic chemical fertilizers.\n3. Molasses can be used for production of ethanol.\nWhich of the above is/are correct?","A":"1 only","B":"2 and 3 only","C":"1 and 3 only","D":"1, 2 and 3","ans":"C","category":"Economy","subcategory":"Agriculture & Allied","tags":["Sugar Industry","Bagasse","Molasses","UPSC","2013"],"expl":"Bagasse is the fibrous residue left after extracting juice from sugarcane and can be burned as biomass fuel.\nMolasses is a by-product of sugar production and is an important feedstock for ethanol production.\nStatement 2 is incorrect.","revisionPoints":"Bagasse → biomass energy\nMolasses → ethanol production\nSugar industry by-products can support value addition.\nBioethanol can be blended with petrol as part of fuel diversification."},
{"no":14,"text":"Variations in the length of daytime and nighttime from season to season are due to:","A":"Earth's rotation on its axis","B":"Earth's revolution around the Sun in an elliptical manner","C":"Latitudinal position of the place","D":"Revolution of the Earth on a tilted axis","ans":"D","category":"Geography","subcategory":"Physical Geography","tags":["Daytime and Nighttime Variation","UPSC","2013"],"expl":"Earth's axis is tilted approximately 23.5° relative to its orbital plane.\nAs Earth revolves around the Sun, this tilt causes different hemispheres to receive varying amounts of sunlight.\nThis produces seasonal variation in day and night duration.","revisionPoints":"Axial tilt + revolution → seasons\nEarth's axis → approximately 23.5° tilt.\nRotation → causes day and night.\nRevolution combined with axial tilt → causes seasonal changes."},
{"no":15,"text":"The Narmada River flows to the west, while most other large Peninsular rivers flow east. Why?\n1. It occupies a linear rift valley.\n2. It flows between the Vindhyas and the Satpuras.\n3. The land slopes to the west from Central India.","A":"1 only","B":"2 and 3","C":"1 and 3","D":"None","ans":"A","category":"Geography","subcategory":"Physical Geography","tags":["Narmada River","Rift Valley","UPSC","2013"],"expl":"The Narmada flows through a rift valley between the Vindhya and Satpura ranges.\nThe rift-valley structure strongly controls its westward course.\nThe source identifies statement 1 as the correct explanation.","revisionPoints":"Narmada → west-flowing Peninsular river\nFlows through a rift valley.\nEmpties into the Arabian Sea.\nTapi/Tapti is another major west-flowing Peninsular river.\nMost major Peninsular rivers flow toward the Bay of Bengal."},
{"no":16,"text":"On planet Earth, most freshwater exists as ice caps and glaciers. Out of the remaining freshwater, the largest proportion:","A":"Is found in the atmosphere as moisture and clouds","B":"Is found in freshwater lakes and rivers","C":"Exists as groundwater","D":"Exists as soil moisture","ans":"C","category":"Geography","subcategory":"Physical Geography","tags":["Freshwater Distribution","Groundwater","UPSC","2013"],"expl":"A large majority of Earth's freshwater is stored in ice caps and glaciers.\nOf the remaining freshwater, groundwater forms the largest reservoir.\nSurface water in rivers and lakes represents only a small fraction of Earth's freshwater.","revisionPoints":"Major freshwater reservoirs:\nIce caps and glaciers\nGroundwater\nLakes and rivers\nSoil moisture and atmosphere\nGroundwater → largest freshwater store after glaciers/ice caps.\nRivers contain only a very small fraction of global freshwater."},
{"no":17,"text":"Consider the following pairs:\n1. Nokrek Biosphere Reserve — Garo Hills\n2. Loktak Lake — Barail Range\n3. Namdapha National Park — Dafla Hills\nWhich of the above pairs is/are correctly matched?","A":"1 only","B":"2 and 3 only","C":"1, 2 and 3","D":"None","ans":"A","category":"Environment & Ecology","subcategory":"Biodiversity","tags":["Nokrek Biosphere Reserve","Loktak Lake","UPSC","2013"],"expl":"Nokrek Biosphere Reserve is located in the Garo Hills of Meghalaya.\nLoktak Lake is in Manipur, but is not associated with the Barail Range as stated.\nNamdapha National Park is in Arunachal Pradesh and is associated with the Patkai/Naga hills region, not Dafla Hills.","revisionPoints":"Nokrek → Garo Hills, Meghalaya\nLoktak Lake → Manipur\nNamdapha → Arunachal Pradesh\nNortheast India has major biodiversity hotspots and protected areas."},
{"no":18,"text":"Which of the following are responsible for bringing dynamic changes on the surface of the Earth?\n1. Electromagnetic radiation\n2. Geothermal energy\n3. Gravitational force\n4. Plate movements\n5. Rotation of the Earth\n6. Revolution of the Earth","A":"1, 2, 3 and 4 only","B":"1, 3, 5 and 6 only","C":"2, 4, 5 and 6 only","D":"1, 2, 3, 4, 5 and 6","ans":"D","category":"Geography","subcategory":"Physical Geography","tags":["Earth Surface Processes","UPSC","2013"],"expl":"Earth's surface is influenced by both internal and external forces.\nSolar electromagnetic radiation drives many surface processes.\nGeothermal energy and plate movements drive internal geological processes.\nGravity, rotation and revolution also influence Earth's physical systems.","revisionPoints":"Endogenic forces → internal energy of Earth\nExogenic forces → mainly solar energy and atmospheric processes\nPlate tectonics → earthquakes, volcanism and mountain building.\nGravity influences erosion, sediment movement and mass wasting.\nEarth's movements influence atmospheric and oceanic processes."},
{"no":19,"text":"Which of the following bodies do not find mention in the Constitution of India?\n1. National Development Council\n2. Planning Commission\n3. Zonal Councils","A":"1 and 2 only","B":"2 only","C":"1 and 3 only","D":"1, 2 and 3","ans":"D","category":"Polity & Governance","subcategory":"Government Bodies","tags":["National Development Council","Planning Commission","UPSC","2013"],"expl":"The Planning Commission was an executive body created by government resolution.\nThe National Development Council was also not a constitutional body.\nZonal Councils were established under the States Reorganisation Act, 1956, not directly by the Constitution.\nTherefore, none of the three is mentioned as a constitutional body in the Constitution.","revisionPoints":"Planning Commission → executive body\nNational Development Council → non-constitutional body\nZonal Councils → statutory bodies\nConstitutional bodies derive their authority directly from the Constitution."},
{"no":20,"text":"The demand for the Tebhaga Peasant Movement in Bengal was for:","A":"Reduction of the landlords' share from one-half to one-third","B":"Grant of ownership of land to peasants","C":"Abolition of Zamindari and serfdom","D":"Writing off all peasant debts","ans":"A","category":"History","subcategory":"Modern India","tags":["Tebhaga Movement","UPSC","2013"],"expl":"The Tebhaga Movement was a peasant movement in Bengal.\nSharecroppers demanded that they should retain two-thirds of the produce.\nThe landlord's share was therefore to be reduced from one-half to one-third.\nThe movement was particularly important in the context of agrarian relations in Bengal.","revisionPoints":"Tebhaga → Bengal\nTebhaga literally refers to two-thirds share.\nSharecroppers demanded 2/3 of the produce.\nIt was an important peasant movement in the 1940s.\nClosely connected with agrarian struggles and the sharecropping system."},
{"no":21,"text":"Parliament can make law for implementing international treaties:","A":"Only with the consent of all the States","B":"Only with the consent of the majority of States","C":"Only with the consent of the States concerned","D":"Without the consent of any State","ans":"D","category":"Polity & Governance","subcategory":"Centre-State Relations","tags":["UPSC","2013"],"expl":"Parliament has the power to make laws for implementing international treaties and agreements.\nThis power is available even when the subject matter falls within the State List.\nState consent is therefore not constitutionally required.\nThis provision ensures that India can fulfil its international obligations through domestic legislation.","revisionPoints":"Article 253 → Legislation for implementing international treaties and agreements.\nParliament can legislate on such matters irrespective of the State List.\nInternational obligations can therefore expand the legislative scope of Parliament.\nState consent is not mandatory."},
{"no":22,"text":"Why do trees in grasslands not replace grasses as the dominant vegetation?","A":"Trees cannot survive in warm climates","B":"Grasses prevent tree growth completely","C":"Water limitations and fire restrict tree growth","D":"Grasslands always have frozen soil","ans":"C","category":"Environment & Ecology","subcategory":"Ecosystems","tags":["UPSC","2013"],"expl":"Grasslands receive enough rainfall to support grasses but generally not enough to support dense forests.\nPeriodic fires can destroy young trees and woody vegetation.\nThese factors help maintain grasses as the dominant vegetation.\nThe balance between rainfall, fire and vegetation determines the ecosystem structure.","revisionPoints":"Grasslands are dominated primarily by grasses and herbaceous plants.\nWater availability influences forest versus grassland formation.\nFire is an important ecological factor in many grasslands.\nSavannas represent an intermediate ecosystem where grasses coexist with scattered trees."},
{"no":23,"text":"Arrange the following ecosystems in decreasing order of productivity:\n1. Mangroves\n2. Grasslands\n3. Lakes\n4. Oceans","A":"Oceans → Lakes → Grasslands → Mangroves","B":"Grasslands → Mangroves → Oceans → Lakes","C":"Mangroves → Grasslands → Lakes → Oceans","D":"Lakes → Mangroves → Grasslands → Oceans","ans":"C","category":"Environment & Ecology","subcategory":"Ecosystems","tags":["UPSC","2013"],"expl":"Mangrove ecosystems have very high primary productivity.\nGrasslands generally have higher productivity than lakes and open oceans.\nOpen oceans have relatively low productivity per unit area because nutrients can be limiting.\nProductivity refers to the rate at which organic matter is produced.","revisionPoints":"Primary productivity → rate of biomass production by producers.\nMangroves are among the highly productive ecosystems.\nProductivity depends on light, nutrients, water and temperature.\nOpen ocean productivity is generally low per unit area."},
{"no":24,"text":"Consider the following statements regarding contour bunding:\n1. It helps reduce soil erosion.\n2. It conserves soil moisture.\n3. It is useful in areas with suitable slopes.\nWhich of the above statements is/are correct?","A":"1 only","B":"2 only","C":"1 and 2 only","D":"None of the above","ans":"D","category":"Geography","subcategory":"Agricultural Geography","tags":["UPSC","2013"],"expl":"The source identifies option (d) as the answer.\nContour-based land management is associated with controlling runoff and soil erosion.\nThe question's answer should therefore be followed according to the provided source.","revisionPoints":"Contour techniques are related to slope management.\nThey are generally associated with controlling surface runoff.\nSoil and water conservation measures vary according to slope, rainfall and soil characteristics.\nFor this question, retain the source-provided answer: (d)."},
{"no":25,"text":"Which of the following is NOT an objective of the Panchayats (Extension to Scheduled Areas) Act, 1996 (PESA)?","A":"Empowering Gram Sabhas","B":"Protecting traditional rights of tribal communities","C":"Creating autonomous regions in tribal areas","D":"Strengthening local self-governance","ans":"C","category":"Polity & Governance","subcategory":"Tribal Welfare","tags":["UPSC","2013"],"expl":"PESA extends the Panchayati Raj framework to Scheduled Areas.\nIt seeks to strengthen the role of Gram Sabhas.\nIt recognises traditional community rights and customs.\nCreation of autonomous regions is not the objective of PESA.","revisionPoints":"PESA Act, 1996 → Panchayati Raj in Scheduled Areas.\nGram Sabha has an important role under PESA.\nPESA seeks to protect customs, traditions and community resources.\nIt strengthens tribal participation in local governance."},
{"no":26,"text":"Under the Forest Rights Act, 2006, the process of determining forest rights is initiated by:","A":"District Collector","B":"State Forest Department","C":"Ministry of Environment","D":"Gram Sabha","ans":"D","category":"Environment & Ecology","subcategory":"Environmental Law","tags":["UPSC","2013"],"expl":"The Gram Sabha plays the foundational role in identifying and determining individual and community forest rights.\nClaims are examined through the institutional process established under the Forest Rights Act.\nThis gives local communities an important role in recognising forest rights.\nThe Act covers rights of eligible forest-dwelling communities.","revisionPoints":"Forest Rights Act, 2006 → recognition of forest rights.\nGram Sabha is the first-level authority in the claims process.\nCovers individual and community forest rights.\nParticularly important for forest-dwelling Scheduled Tribes and other traditional forest dwellers."},
{"no":27,"text":"Aflatoxins are primarily produced by:","A":"Bacteria","B":"Viruses","C":"Moulds","D":"Protozoans","ans":"C","category":"Science & Technology","subcategory":"Biology","tags":["UPSC","2013"],"expl":"Aflatoxins are toxic substances produced by certain species of moulds.\nThey can contaminate food and agricultural commodities.\nImproper storage under favourable conditions for fungal growth increases contamination risk.\nAflatoxins are important from a food-safety perspective.","revisionPoints":"Aflatoxins are mycotoxins.\nThey are associated with certain fungi/moulds.\nFood contamination can occur in improperly stored crops.\nThey are significant because of their toxicity."},
{"no":28,"text":"Economic justice in the Indian Constitution is reflected mainly in:","A":"Fundamental Rights only","B":"Preamble and Directive Principles of State Policy","C":"Fundamental Duties only","D":"Emergency provisions","ans":"B","category":"Polity & Governance","subcategory":"Constitution","tags":["UPSC","2013"],"expl":"The Preamble promises economic justice.\nThe Directive Principles provide directions for reducing economic inequalities.\nThey seek to promote a more equitable social and economic order.\nTogether, these provisions establish the constitutional framework for economic justice.","revisionPoints":"Preamble → explicitly mentions economic justice.\nDPSP → promote social and economic justice.\nArticle 38 → promotion of welfare and reduction of inequalities.\nArticle 39 → principles relating to distribution of material resources and economic justice."},
{"no":29,"text":"Which of the following set of pollutants is associated with electronic waste?","A":"Nitrogen, oxygen, hydrogen, carbon","B":"Beryllium, cadmium, chromium, mercury and lead","C":"Calcium, sodium, potassium and magnesium","D":"Helium, neon, argon and krypton","ans":"B","category":"Environment & Ecology","subcategory":"Pollution","tags":["UPSC","2013"],"expl":"Electronic equipment contains several metals and hazardous substances.\nImproper disposal of e-waste can release toxic elements into the environment.\nHeavy metals can contaminate soil and water.\nInformal recycling and unsafe disposal can increase exposure risks.","revisionPoints":"E-waste → discarded electrical and electronic equipment.\nImportant hazardous elements include lead, mercury, cadmium and chromium.\nImproper disposal can cause soil and water contamination.\nSafe collection, recycling and disposal are essential."},
{"no":30,"text":"Acid rain is mainly associated with atmospheric pollutants such as:","A":"Carbon monoxide and methane","B":"Carbon dioxide and oxygen","C":"Methane and ozone","D":"Nitrous oxide and sulphur dioxide","ans":"D","category":"Environment & Ecology","subcategory":"Pollution","tags":["UPSC","2013"],"expl":"The source identifies nitrous oxide and sulphur dioxide as the relevant pollutants.\nSulphur compounds and nitrogen oxides can undergo atmospheric reactions.\nThese reactions produce acidic compounds that can return to the surface through precipitation.\nAcid deposition can affect aquatic ecosystems, soils and vegetation.","revisionPoints":"Acid rain → acidic precipitation/deposition.\nSulphur dioxide is an important precursor.\nNitrogen oxides also contribute to acid deposition.\nEffects include damage to aquatic ecosystems, soil and vegetation."},
{"no":31,"text":"Consider the following statements about food chains:\n1. Energy decreases as we move from one trophic level to the next.\n2. The number of organisms always increases at higher trophic levels.\n3. Decomposers are not part of ecosystem nutrient cycling.\nWhich of the statements given above is/are correct?","A":"1 only","B":"1 and 2 only","C":"2 and 3 only","D":"1, 2 and 3","ans":"A","category":"Environment & Ecology","subcategory":"Ecology","tags":["UPSC","2013"],"expl":"Energy is progressively lost as heat during transfer between trophic levels.\nTherefore, available energy decreases at higher trophic levels.\nThe number of organisms does not always increase at higher trophic levels.\nDecomposers are essential for nutrient cycling.","revisionPoints":"Trophic level → position occupied by an organism in a food chain.\nEnergy transfer between trophic levels is inefficient.\nDecomposers recycle nutrients back into ecosystems.\nFood chains generally begin with producers."},
{"no":32,"text":"Which of the following pairs are incorrectly matched?\n1. Corbett National Park — Ganga\n2. Kaziranga National Park — Manas\n3. Silent Valley National Park — Kaveri","A":"1 only","B":"2 only","C":"3 only","D":"1, 2 and 3","ans":"D","category":"Environment & Ecology","subcategory":"National Parks & Sanctuaries","tags":["UPSC","2013"],"expl":"The source considers all three pairs incorrectly matched.\nThe question tests the association between protected areas and rivers.\nSuch geographical associations are useful for understanding India's protected-area network.\nAll three statements therefore fall under the incorrect category according to the source.","revisionPoints":"Corbett National Park → Uttarakhand.\nKaziranga National Park → Assam.\nSilent Valley National Park → Kerala.\nProtected areas and their geographical surroundings are important mapping topics."},
{"no":33,"text":"Which of the following is a biofertilizer?\n1. Agaricus\n2. Nostoc\n3. Spirogyra","A":"Agaricus only","B":"Nostoc only","C":"Agaricus and Spirogyra","D":"All three","ans":"B","category":"Science & Technology","subcategory":"Biology","tags":["UPSC","2013"],"expl":"Nostoc is a cyanobacterium capable of fixing atmospheric nitrogen.\nNitrogen fixation can increase nitrogen availability in agricultural systems.\nAgaricus is a mushroom.\nSpirogyra is a filamentous green alga and is not the biofertilizer identified by the source.","revisionPoints":"Nostoc → nitrogen-fixing cyanobacterium.\nCyanobacteria can contribute to biological nitrogen fixation.\nBiofertilizers use living organisms to improve nutrient availability.\nImportant examples include Rhizobium and certain cyanobacteria."},
{"no":34,"text":"Which of the following processes add nitrogen to the soil?\n1. Excretion of urea by animals\n2. Burning of coal\n3. Death of vegetation","A":"1 only","B":"2 only","C":"1 and 3 only","D":"1, 2 and 3","ans":"D","category":"Environment & Ecology","subcategory":"Ecology","tags":["UPSC","2013"],"expl":"Urea excretion contributes nitrogen-containing compounds to the environment.\nDecomposition of dead vegetation returns nitrogen to the soil.\nThe source also includes coal burning in the given set.\nThese processes are considered in the question as contributing nitrogen to the environment/soil.","revisionPoints":"Nitrogen is continuously exchanged through the nitrogen cycle.\nOrganic matter decomposition returns nutrients to soil.\nAnimal excreta can contribute nitrogen compounds.\nHuman activities can alter natural nutrient cycles."},
{"no":35,"text":"The lion-tailed macaque is naturally found in:","A":"Tamil Nadu, Kerala and Karnataka","B":"Rajasthan, Gujarat and Madhya Pradesh","C":"Assam, Bihar and Odisha","D":"Punjab, Haryana and Uttar Pradesh","ans":"A","category":"Environment & Ecology","subcategory":"Biodiversity","tags":["UPSC","2013"],"expl":"The lion-tailed macaque is associated with the Western Ghats.\nIts natural distribution includes parts of Tamil Nadu, Kerala and Karnataka.\nIt is closely associated with evergreen forest habitats.\nHabitat loss and fragmentation are major conservation concerns.","revisionPoints":"Lion-tailed macaque → Western Ghats.\nImportant habitat → tropical evergreen forests.\nStates include Kerala, Karnataka and Tamil Nadu.\nIt is an important species for Western Ghats biodiversity conservation."},
{"no":36,"text":"Consider the following statements:\n1. Chaitya refers to a place of worship.\n2. Vihara refers to a residence or dwelling place for monks.\nWhich of the statements given above is/are correct?","A":"1 only","B":"1 and 2","C":"2 only","D":"Neither 1 nor 2","ans":"B","category":"Art & Culture","subcategory":"Architecture","tags":["UPSC","2013"],"expl":"A Chaitya was primarily a Buddhist prayer or worship hall.\nA Vihara was a monastery or residential complex for monks.\nBoth terms are important in the study of Buddhist architecture.\nBuddhist cave complexes often contained both chaityas and viharas.","revisionPoints":"Chaitya → prayer/worship hall.\nVihara → monastery/residence of monks.\nBoth are important elements of Buddhist architecture.\nExamples are found in ancient rock-cut cave complexes."},
{"no":37,"text":"In Buddhism, Nirvana primarily refers to:","A":"Extinction of the flame of desire","B":"Attainment of political power","C":"Rebirth into a higher caste","D":"Worship of nature","ans":"A","category":"Art & Culture","subcategory":"Religion & Philosophy","tags":["UPSC","2013"],"expl":"Nirvana represents liberation from suffering and the cycle of rebirth.\nIt involves overcoming desire, attachment and ignorance.\nThe extinction of desire is central to the Buddhist understanding of liberation.\nIt represents the culmination of the Buddhist spiritual path.","revisionPoints":"Nirvana → liberation from suffering and the cycle of rebirth.\nDesire/craving is a major cause of suffering in Buddhist thought.\nThe Four Noble Truths explain suffering and its cessation.\nThe Eightfold Path provides the path toward cessation of suffering."},
{"no":38,"text":"Which of the following is described as fundamental in the governance of the country?","A":"Fundamental Rights","B":"Fundamental Duties","C":"Directive Principles of State Policy","D":"Emergency Provisions","ans":"C","category":"Polity & Governance","subcategory":"Directive Principles","tags":["UPSC","2013"],"expl":"Article 37 states that the Directive Principles are fundamental in the governance of the country.\nThey guide the State in making laws and policies.\nThey are not enforceable by courts.\nThey aim to establish social and economic justice.","revisionPoints":"DPSP → Part IV of the Constitution.\nArticle 37 → fundamental in governance.\nDPSPs are non-justiciable.\nThey guide the State toward a welfare state."},
{"no":39,"text":"The Simon Commission was opposed in India mainly because:","A":"It proposed abolition of provincial governments","B":"It demanded complete independence immediately","C":"It had no Indian member","D":"It opposed constitutional reforms","ans":"C","category":"History","subcategory":"Freedom Movement","tags":["UPSC","2013"],"expl":"The Simon Commission was appointed by the British government to examine constitutional reforms in India.\nIt consisted entirely of British members.\nIndian political groups strongly objected to the absence of Indian representation.\nThe arrival of the Commission led to widespread protests.","revisionPoints":"Simon Commission → 1927.\nChairman → Sir John Simon.\nIt had no Indian member.\nPopular slogan → “Simon Go Back.”\nLala Lajpat Rai was injured during a protest against the Commission."},
{"no":40,"text":"The Quit India Movement was launched in response to:","A":"Cabinet Mission proposals","B":"Cripps Proposals","C":"Mountbatten Plan","D":"Simon Commission Report","ans":"B","category":"History","subcategory":"Freedom Movement","tags":["UPSC","2013"],"expl":"The Cripps Mission came to India during World War II.\nIts proposals failed to satisfy the major demands of Indian nationalists.\nThe failure of the mission contributed to growing political dissatisfaction.\nThe Quit India Movement was launched by the Indian National Congress in August 1942.","revisionPoints":"Cripps Mission → 1942.\nQuit India Movement → August 1942.\nAssociated with the slogan “Do or Die.”\nMahatma Gandhi played the central leadership role.\nThe movement demanded an end to British rule in India."},
{"no":41,"text":"Balance of Payments (BoP) records:","A":"All import and export transactions during a given period","B":"Only visible exports and imports","C":"Only capital transactions","D":"Only foreign exchange transactions of the government","ans":"A","category":"Economy","subcategory":"External Sector","tags":["UPSC","2013"],"expl":"Balance of Payments records a country's economic transactions with the rest of the world.\nIt covers transactions involving goods, services, income and transfers, along with capital and financial transactions.\nIt is maintained for a specified period.\nThe source identifies option (a) as the answer.","revisionPoints":"BoP → systematic record of economic transactions with the rest of the world.\nIncludes current account and capital/financial transactions.\nIt is different from the Balance of Trade, which focuses mainly on merchandise exports and imports.\nBoP is generally presented for a specific accounting period."},
{"no":42,"text":"The Reserve Bank of India regulates commercial banks with regard to:\n1. Liquidity of assets\n2. Branch expansion\n3. Merger of banks\n4. Winding-up of banks","A":"1 and 2 only","B":"2 and 3 only","C":"1, 2 and 3 only","D":"1, 2, 3 and 4","ans":"D","category":"Economy","subcategory":"Banking & Monetary Policy","tags":["UPSC","2013"],"expl":"RBI exercises regulatory and supervisory functions over commercial banks.\nIts functions include regulating banking liquidity and branch expansion.\nRBI is also involved in matters relating to bank mergers and winding-up.\nThese powers form part of India's banking regulatory framework.","revisionPoints":"RBI is India's central banking and banking regulatory institution.\nIt regulates and supervises commercial banks.\nImportant areas include liquidity, branches and banking operations.\nRBI also has regulatory roles concerning restructuring and winding-up of banks."},
{"no":43,"text":"An increase in the Bank Rate generally indicates:","A":"Easy money policy","B":"Expansionary monetary policy","C":"Increase in money supply","D":"Tight money policy","ans":"D","category":"Economy","subcategory":"Banking & Monetary Policy","tags":["UPSC","2013"],"expl":"The Bank Rate is a monetary policy instrument associated with the RBI.\nAn increase in the rate makes borrowing more expensive.\nHigher borrowing costs can reduce credit expansion.\nTherefore, an increase in the Bank Rate is associated with a tight money policy.","revisionPoints":"Bank Rate → rate associated with RBI lending/rediscounting framework.\nHigher rates generally make credit more expensive.\nTight monetary policy aims to restrain excessive liquidity and inflationary pressure.\nExpansionary policy generally works in the opposite direction."},
{"no":44,"text":"Deficit financing is generally used for:","A":"Economic development","B":"Reducing exports","C":"Reducing employment","D":"Restricting investment","ans":"A","category":"Economy","subcategory":"Public Finance","tags":["UPSC","2013"],"expl":"Deficit financing involves financing government expenditure beyond available revenues through specified borrowing or money-creation mechanisms.\nIt can be used to finance development expenditure.\nGovernments may use deficit financing to support infrastructure and economic activity.\nExcessive deficit financing can, however, create inflationary pressures.","revisionPoints":"Fiscal deficit → excess of total expenditure over receipts excluding borrowings.\nDeficit financing can support development expenditure.\nExcessive monetary financing may contribute to inflation.\nBorrowing is an important source of financing government deficits."},
{"no":45,"text":"Consider the following statements regarding the Indus Valley Civilization:\n1. People worshipped male deities.\n2. People worshipped female deities.\n3. They worshipped only animals and trees.\nWhich of the above is/are correct?","A":"1 only","B":"1 and 2 only","C":"2 and 3 only","D":"1, 2 and 3","ans":"B","category":"History","subcategory":"Ancient India","tags":["UPSC","2013"],"expl":"Archaeological evidence indicates the presence of both male and female divine figures.\nFemale figurines have often been associated with fertility-related interpretations.\nMale deity representations have also been identified.\nThe source considers statements 1 and 2 correct.","revisionPoints":"Indus Valley Civilization → Bronze Age urban civilization.\nImportant sites include Harappa, Mohenjo-daro, Dholavira and Lothal.\nReligious evidence includes figurines, seals and symbolic objects.\nInterpretations of Harappan religious practices are based primarily on archaeological evidence."},
{"no":46,"text":"Tattooing with contaminated equipment can transmit:\n1. Hepatitis B\n2. HIV/AIDS\n3. Malaria\n4. Dengue","A":"1 only","B":"1 and 2 only","C":"2 and 3 only","D":"1, 2, 3 and 4","ans":"B","category":"Science & Technology","subcategory":"Health & Diseases","tags":["UPSC","2013"],"expl":"Reuse of contaminated needles can expose a person to blood-borne infections.\nHepatitis B can be transmitted through infected blood.\nHIV can also be transmitted through contaminated needles and blood exposure.\nProper sterilisation and single-use equipment reduce such risks.","revisionPoints":"Hepatitis B → viral infection affecting the liver.\nHIV → virus that attacks the immune system.\nContaminated needles are an important route for blood-borne infections.\nSterile/single-use tattoo equipment reduces transmission risk."},
{"no":47,"text":"Consider the following statements regarding Jainism:\n1. Jainism rejected the authority of the Vedas.\n2. Jainism accepted the caste system as divinely ordained.\n3. Jainism emphasised non-violence.\nWhich of the statements given above is/are correct?","A":"1 only","B":"2 only","C":"1 and 3 only","D":"1, 2 and 3","ans":"C","category":"Art & Culture","subcategory":"Religion & Philosophy","tags":["UPSC","2013"],"expl":"Jainism did not accept the authority of the Vedas in the manner of orthodox Vedic traditions.\nAhimsa, or non-violence, is a central principle of Jain philosophy.\nJainism emphasised ethical conduct, self-discipline and liberation.\nThe source considers statements 1 and 3 correct.","revisionPoints":"Jainism → one of the major śramaṇa traditions of ancient India.\nAhimsa → central ethical principle.\nOther important principles include Aparigraha and Anekantavada.\nMahavira is regarded as the 24th Tirthankara."},
{"no":48,"text":"The combination of physical space and the functional role of an organism in an ecosystem is called:","A":"Habitat","B":"Ecological niche","C":"Biome","D":"Biosphere","ans":"B","category":"Environment & Ecology","subcategory":"Ecology","tags":["UPSC","2013"],"expl":"A habitat is the physical place where an organism lives.\nAn ecological niche includes the organism's functional role and interactions within the ecosystem.\nIt therefore includes both spatial and functional aspects.\nDifferent species can sometimes occupy different niches within the same habitat.","revisionPoints":"Habitat → place where an organism lives.\nNiche → functional role and ecological position of an organism.\nBiome → large ecological region characterised by climate and vegetation.\nBiosphere → global zone of life."},
{"no":49,"text":"Photochemical smog is formed through reactions involving:","A":"NO₂, O₃ and PAN in sunlight","B":"CO₂ and water vapour","C":"Sulphur dioxide and nitrogen gas","D":"Methane and carbon dioxide in darkness","ans":"A","category":"Environment & Ecology","subcategory":"Pollution","tags":["UPSC","2013"],"expl":"Photochemical smog develops through chemical reactions driven by sunlight.\nNitrogen oxides participate in these atmospheric reactions.\nOzone and PAN (peroxyacetyl nitrate) are important secondary pollutants.\nVehicle emissions are a major source of precursor pollutants in urban areas.","revisionPoints":"Photochemical smog → sunlight-driven atmospheric pollution.\nMajor precursors → NOx and volatile organic compounds (VOCs).\nImportant secondary pollutants → ozone and PAN.\nCommon in areas with intense sunlight and heavy vehicular emissions."},
{"no":50,"text":"Which minerals are important for muscle contraction?","A":"Iron and iodine","B":"Potassium and iron","C":"Calcium and sodium","D":"Zinc and iodine","ans":"C","category":"Science & Technology","subcategory":"Biology","tags":["UPSC","2013"],"expl":"Calcium plays a central role in the mechanism of muscle contraction.\nSodium is important in generating electrical signals in nerve and muscle cells.\nProper electrolyte balance is essential for normal muscle function.\nThe source identifies calcium and sodium as the answer.","revisionPoints":"Calcium → important for muscle contraction and bone health.\nSodium → important for nerve impulses and fluid balance.\nPotassium is also important for normal nerve and muscle function.\nElectrolyte imbalance can affect neuromuscular activity."},
{"no":51,"text":"Consider the following statements about the Public Accounts Committee:\n1. It examines the reports of the Comptroller and Auditor General.\n2. It consists only of members of the Lok Sabha.\n3. A minister cannot be a member of the Committee.\nWhich of the statements given above is/are correct?","A":"1 only","B":"2 and 3 only","C":"1 and 3 only","D":"1, 2 and 3","ans":"B","category":"Polity & Governance","subcategory":"Parliamentary Committees","tags":["UPSC","2013"],"expl":"The source identifies statements 2 and 3 as correct.\nThe Public Accounts Committee is a parliamentary financial committee.\nMinisters are not eligible to be members of the committee.\nThe committee performs important financial scrutiny functions.","revisionPoints":"Public Accounts Committee (PAC) → important financial committee of Parliament.\nIt examines public expenditure and related financial accountability.\nMinisters cannot be members of the PAC.\nParliamentary committees strengthen legislative oversight."},
{"no":52,"text":"Which pair of Bhakti saints was associated with the period when the Lodi dynasty ended and Babur established Mughal rule?","A":"Kabir and Surdas","B":"Tulsidas and Mirabai","C":"Chaitanya and Namdev","D":"Dadu Dayal and Guru Nanak","ans":"D","category":"Art & Culture","subcategory":"Religion & Philosophy","tags":["UPSC","2013"],"expl":"The question places the saints in the historical period of transition from the Lodi dynasty to Mughal rule.\nGuru Nanak was the founder of the Sikh tradition.\nDadu Dayal was an important Bhakti saint.\nThe source identifies the pair in option (d).","revisionPoints":"Guru Nanak → founder of Sikhism.\nDadu Dayal → Bhakti saint associated with the Nirguna tradition.\nBhakti traditions emphasised devotion and spiritual equality in different forms.\nThe period witnessed significant religious and social movements."},
{"no":53,"text":"Which of the following are decomposers?","A":"Green plants and algae","B":"Fungi and bacteria","C":"Herbivores and carnivores","D":"Birds and reptiles","ans":"B","category":"Environment & Ecology","subcategory":"Ecology","tags":["UPSC","2013"],"expl":"Decomposers break down dead organic matter.\nFungi and bacteria are major decomposers in ecosystems.\nThey release nutrients back into the environment.\nThis makes them essential for nutrient cycling.","revisionPoints":"Major decomposers → bacteria and fungi.\nThey break down dead organic matter.\nDecomposition returns nutrients to soil and ecosystems.\nDecomposers are essential components of ecosystem functioning."},
{"no":54,"text":"Major fishing grounds are generally found where:","A":"Two warm currents meet","B":"Two cold currents meet","C":"Warm and cold ocean currents meet","D":"Ocean currents are completely absent","ans":"C","category":"Geography","subcategory":"Oceanography","tags":["UPSC","2013"],"expl":"The meeting of warm and cold currents can create favourable conditions for marine productivity.\nSuch regions tend to have abundant nutrients.\nNutrient availability supports plankton growth.\nPlankton supports larger marine food chains and fishing activity.","revisionPoints":"Major fishing grounds are often associated with converging ocean currents.\nUpwelling can also bring nutrient-rich deep water to the surface.\nHigh plankton productivity supports fish populations.\nExamples include important fishing regions of the North Atlantic and Pacific."},
{"no":55,"text":"Which of the following are characteristics of equatorial forests?\n1. High temperature throughout the year\n2. Heavy rainfall\n3. Dense evergreen vegetation","A":"1 only","B":"1 and 2 only","C":"2 and 3 only","D":"1, 2 and 3","ans":"D","category":"Geography","subcategory":"World Geography","tags":["UPSC","2013"],"expl":"Equatorial regions receive high solar radiation throughout the year.\nRainfall is generally high and distributed across much of the year.\nWarm and humid conditions support dense evergreen forests.\nThese forests contain extremely high biodiversity.","revisionPoints":"Equatorial climate → high temperature and rainfall.\nVegetation → predominantly evergreen forests.\nHigh biodiversity is a major characteristic.\nForests often have multiple vertical layers."},
{"no":56,"text":"Which of the following are components of the capital account/financial flows?\n1. Foreign loans\n2. Foreign Direct Investment\n3. Portfolio investment","A":"1 only","B":"1, 2 and 3","C":"2 and 3 only","D":"1 and 3 only","ans":"B","category":"Economy","subcategory":"External Sector","tags":["UPSC","2013"],"expl":"Foreign loans represent external financial borrowing.\nFDI involves foreign investment in productive assets or businesses.\nPortfolio investment involves investment in financial securities.\nThese represent international financial flows affecting a country's external accounts.","revisionPoints":"FDI → foreign investment involving a lasting interest/control.\nPortfolio investment → investment in financial assets/securities.\nForeign loans are external sources of financing.\nCapital/financial flows affect a country's external financial position."},
{"no":57,"text":"Mural paintings are particularly associated with:\n1. Ajanta\n2. Lepakshi","A":"Ajanta only","B":"Ajanta and Lepakshi","C":"Lepakshi only","D":"Neither Ajanta nor Lepakshi","ans":"B","category":"Art & Culture","subcategory":"Painting","tags":["UPSC","2013"],"expl":"Ajanta is famous for its ancient Buddhist murals.\nLepakshi is known for its Vijayanagara-period temple paintings.\nBoth represent important traditions of Indian mural painting.\nTheir themes, styles and historical contexts differ.","revisionPoints":"Ajanta → Buddhist caves and murals.\nLepakshi → Vijayanagara-period art.\nIndian murals depict religious, social and cultural themes.\nAjanta paintings are particularly important for studying ancient Indian painting traditions."},
{"no":58,"text":"Which of the following statements about Sankhya philosophy is correct?","A":"Ritual sacrifice alone leads to liberation","B":"Self-knowledge leads to liberation","C":"Political power leads to liberation","D":"Material wealth leads to liberation","ans":"B","category":"Art & Culture","subcategory":"Religion & Philosophy","tags":["UPSC","2013"],"expl":"Sankhya is based on the distinction between Purusha and Prakriti.\nLiberation results from correct knowledge of this distinction.\nSelf-knowledge helps the individual overcome identification with material nature.\nThe source identifies option (b) as correct.","revisionPoints":"Sankhya → one of the six orthodox schools of Indian philosophy.\nTwo fundamental principles → Purusha and Prakriti.\nPurusha → consciousness.\nPrakriti → material/natural principle.\nLiberation is associated with discriminative knowledge."},
{"no":59,"text":"Which of the following are characteristics of parliamentary government?\n1. Cabinet members are members of Parliament.\n2. Ministers hold office as long as they enjoy the confidence of the lower house.\n3. The executive is completely independent of the legislature.","A":"1 and 2 only","B":"2 and 3 only","C":"1 and 3 only","D":"1, 2 and 3","ans":"A","category":"Polity & Governance","subcategory":"Parliament","tags":["UPSC","2013"],"expl":"In a parliamentary system, ministers are generally members of the legislature.\nThe Council of Ministers remains in office while enjoying the confidence of the Lok Sabha.\nThe executive is therefore politically responsible to the legislature.\nIt is not completely independent of the legislature.","revisionPoints":"India follows a parliamentary system.\nCouncil of Ministers is collectively responsible to Lok Sabha.\nPrime Minister is the head of the Council of Ministers.\nParliamentary government involves close interaction between executive and legislature."},
{"no":60,"text":"Why do continents generally experience a greater annual range of temperature in their interiors?","A":"Thermal difference between land and water","B":"Greater atmospheric pressure","C":"Greater distance from the equator alone","D":"Greater cloud cover throughout the year","ans":"A","category":"Geography","subcategory":"Climatology","tags":["UPSC","2013"],"expl":"Land heats and cools more rapidly than water.\nLarge continental interiors are far from the moderating influence of oceans.\nTherefore, summers can become very hot and winters very cold.\nCoastal areas generally experience a smaller annual temperature range because of maritime influence.","revisionPoints":"Continentality → greater temperature extremes in continental interiors.\nLand has a lower heat capacity than water.\nOceans moderate temperatures of nearby coastal regions.\nContinental interiors generally have a higher annual temperature range."},
{"no":61,"text":"Which of the following are characteristic features of Indian coal?","A":"High ash and low sulphur content","B":"Low ash and high sulphur content","C":"High ash and high sulphur content","D":"Low ash and low moisture content","ans":"A","category":"Geography","subcategory":"Minerals","tags":["UPSC","2013"],"expl":"Indian coal generally contains a relatively high proportion of ash.\nIts sulphur content is generally comparatively low.\nThe quality of coal varies depending on the coalfield and geological formation.\nCoal remains an important conventional energy resource in India.","revisionPoints":"Indian coal → generally high ash and low sulphur.\nMajor coal deposits occur in Gondwana formations.\nImportant coal-producing states include Jharkhand, Odisha, Chhattisgarh and West Bengal.\nCoal is widely used in thermal power generation."},
{"no":62,"text":"Consider the following statements regarding laterite soils:\n1. They are generally red in colour.\n2. Crops such as tapioca and cashew can grow well in them.\nWhich of the statements given above is/are correct?","A":"1 only","B":"2 only","C":"Both 1 and 2","D":"Neither 1 nor 2","ans":"C","category":"Geography","subcategory":"Physical Geography (India)","tags":["UPSC","2013"],"expl":"Laterite soils commonly develop under conditions of high temperature and heavy rainfall.\nIntense leaching removes many soluble materials.\nIron compounds contribute to their characteristic reddish colour.\nWith suitable management and fertilisation, crops such as cashew and tapioca can be cultivated.","revisionPoints":"Laterite soil → associated with heavy rainfall and intense leaching.\nOften contains significant iron and aluminium compounds.\nCommon in parts of the Western and Eastern Ghats.\nSuitable crops include cashew, tapioca and certain plantation crops."},
{"no":63,"text":"Consider the following pairs:\n1. Natural gas — Gondwana rocks\n2. Mica — Kodarma\n3. Petroleum — Dharwar rocks\nWhich of the pairs given above is/are correctly matched?","A":"1 only","B":"2 only","C":"1 and 2 only","D":"1, 2 and 3","ans":"B","category":"Geography","subcategory":"Minerals","tags":["UPSC","2013"],"expl":"Kodarma is historically well known for its mica deposits.\nThe source considers the association of natural gas with Gondwana rocks incorrect.\nPetroleum is not associated with Dharwar rocks in the manner stated.\nTherefore, only the mica–Kodarma pair is correct according to the source.","revisionPoints":"Kodarma → historically important mica-producing region.\nDharwar rocks → important source of metallic minerals.\nGondwana formations are particularly important for coal deposits.\nMineral resources are closely related to geological formations."},
{"no":64,"text":"Which of the following is a group of Kharif crops?","A":"Wheat, gram and mustard","B":"Barley, peas and linseed","C":"Cotton, groundnut and rice","D":"Wheat, rice and gram","ans":"C","category":"Geography","subcategory":"Agricultural Geography","tags":["UPSC","2013"],"expl":"Kharif crops are generally sown with the onset of the southwest monsoon.\nThey require relatively warm conditions and seasonal rainfall.\nRice, cotton and groundnut are major Kharif crops.\nThey are generally harvested after the monsoon season.","revisionPoints":"Kharif crops → associated with the monsoon season.\nExamples → rice, cotton, maize, groundnut and soybean.\nRabi crops → wheat, gram, mustard and barley.\nKharif agriculture is strongly influenced by monsoon rainfall."},
{"no":65,"text":"Which region is characterised by extreme climate, scanty rainfall and nomadic herding?","A":"Mediterranean region","B":"Central Asian Steppe","C":"Equatorial region","D":"Western European region","ans":"B","category":"Geography","subcategory":"World Geography","tags":["UPSC","2013"],"expl":"The Central Asian Steppe has a strongly continental climate.\nRainfall is generally limited.\nExtensive grasslands support livestock-based pastoral activities.\nNomadic and semi-nomadic herding has historically been important in the region.","revisionPoints":"Steppe → temperate grassland.\nCentral Asian climate → strongly continental.\nTraditional livelihood → pastoralism and livestock herding.\nLow and variable rainfall limits dense forest growth."},
{"no":66,"text":"An unexpected rise in inflation generally benefits:","A":"Debtors","B":"Bondholders","C":"Creditors","D":"Fixed-income earners","ans":"A","category":"Economy","subcategory":"Inflation","tags":["UPSC","2013"],"expl":"Inflation reduces the purchasing power of money.\nA debtor repaying a fixed nominal amount can repay it with money having lower real purchasing power.\nCreditors and fixed-income earners may lose in real terms if income does not adjust with inflation.\nTherefore, unexpected inflation can redistribute wealth from creditors toward debtors.","revisionPoints":"Inflation → sustained increase in the general price level.\nInflation reduces the purchasing power of money.\nUnexpected inflation can benefit debtors.\nFixed-income groups can be adversely affected when incomes do not rise with prices."},
{"no":67,"text":"Disguised unemployment exists when:","A":"Labour productivity is very high","B":"All workers are fully employed","C":"Marginal productivity of labour is zero","D":"Wages are above productivity","ans":"C","category":"Economy","subcategory":"Labour & Employment","tags":["UPSC","2013"],"expl":"Disguised unemployment occurs when more workers are engaged in an activity than are actually required.\nRemoving some workers does not significantly reduce total output.\nTherefore, the marginal productivity of the surplus workers may be zero.\nIt is commonly discussed in relation to agriculture in developing economies.","revisionPoints":"Disguised unemployment → surplus labour with little or no marginal contribution.\nCommonly associated with agriculture and informal sectors.\nRemoving surplus workers may not reduce total production.\nIt differs from open unemployment, where people are willing to work but cannot find employment."},
{"no":68,"text":"Consider the following statements:\n1. Union Ministers hold office during the pleasure of the President.\n2. The Prime Minister has a duty to communicate to the President proposals for legislation.\nWhich of the statements given above is/are correct?","A":"1 only","B":"Both 1 and 2","C":"2 only","D":"Neither 1 nor 2","ans":"B","category":"Polity & Governance","subcategory":"Union Executive","tags":["UPSC","2013"],"expl":"Under the parliamentary system, Union Ministers formally hold office during the pleasure of the President.\nThe Council of Ministers is headed by the Prime Minister.\nThe Prime Minister has constitutional duties regarding communication with the President.\nThese provisions are part of the constitutional relationship between the President and Council of Ministers.","revisionPoints":"Article 75 → provisions relating to Union Ministers.\nMinisters are appointed by the President on the advice of the Prime Minister.\nArticle 78 → duties of the Prime Minister regarding communication with the President.\nCouncil of Ministers is collectively responsible to the Lok Sabha."},
{"no":69,"text":"Consider the following statements:\n1. Economic and social planning is included in the Concurrent List.\n2. Panchayats can be entrusted with responsibilities relating to economic development and social justice.\nWhich of the statements given above is/are correct?","A":"1 only","B":"Both 1 and 2","C":"2 only","D":"Neither 1 nor 2","ans":"B","category":"Polity & Governance","subcategory":"Local Government","tags":["UPSC","2013"],"expl":"Economic and social planning is included in the Concurrent List.\nThe Constitution envisages Panchayats playing a role in local development.\nPanchayats can be entrusted with functions relating to economic development and social justice.\nThis reflects India's decentralised governance framework.","revisionPoints":"Concurrent List → subjects on which both Union and State legislatures can legislate.\nEconomic and social planning is a Concurrent List subject.\n73rd Constitutional Amendment → constitutional status to Panchayats.\nPanchayats can be assigned responsibilities for economic development and social justice."},
{"no":70,"text":"Consider the following statements about nominated members of Parliament:\n1. Nominated members of Parliament can vote in the election of the President.\n2. Nominated members of Parliament can vote in the election of the Vice-President.\nWhich of the statements given above is/are correct?","A":"1 only","B":"2 only","C":"Both 1 and 2","D":"Neither 1 nor 2","ans":"B","category":"Polity & Governance","subcategory":"Parliament","tags":["UPSC","2013"],"expl":"Nominated members of Parliament do not participate in the election of the President.\nThe President is elected by elected members of both Houses of Parliament and elected members of State Legislative Assemblies.\nNominated members of both Houses can vote in the election of the Vice-President.\nTherefore, only statement 2 is correct.","revisionPoints":"President's election → elected MPs + elected MLAs of States.\nNominated MPs do not vote in the Presidential election.\nVice-President's election → members of both Houses of Parliament, including nominated members.\nState Legislative Assembly members do not participate in the Vice-Presidential election."},
{"no":71,"text":"Consider the following statements regarding the National Legal Services Authority (NALSA):\n1. It provides free legal services to eligible sections of society.\n2. It organises Lok Adalats.\nWhich of the statements given above is/are correct?","A":"1 only","B":"2 only","C":"Both 1 and 2","D":"Neither 1 nor 2","ans":"C","category":"Polity & Governance","subcategory":"Judiciary","tags":["UPSC","2013"],"expl":"NALSA works to provide access to justice through legal aid.\nIt provides free legal services to eligible persons under the legal services framework.\nIt also promotes and organises Lok Adalats.\nIts broader objective is to ensure that lack of financial resources does not prevent access to justice.","revisionPoints":"NALSA → National Legal Services Authority.\nEstablished under the Legal Services Authorities Act, 1987.\nProvides free legal aid to eligible persons.\nPromotes Lok Adalats and access to justice."},
{"no":72,"text":"Which of the following does NOT produce thunder?","A":"Rapid expansion of heated air","B":"Sudden cooling and contraction of air","C":"Shock waves produced by lightning","D":"Rotation of the Earth","ans":"D","category":"Geography","subcategory":"Climatology","tags":["UPSC","2013"],"expl":"Thunder is the sound produced by the rapid heating and expansion of air around a lightning channel.\nThe sudden expansion creates pressure disturbances or shock waves.\nThese disturbances propagate through the atmosphere as sound.\nEarth's rotation is not responsible for producing thunder.","revisionPoints":"Lightning → intense electrical discharge.\nLightning rapidly heats surrounding air.\nRapid air expansion produces shock waves.\nThunder is therefore a consequence of lightning's atmospheric effects."},
{"no":73,"text":"Which of the following tribal groups are correctly matched with their States?\n1. Limbu — Sikkim\n2. Dongaria Kondh — Odisha\n3. Toda — Assam\n4. Bodo — Kerala","A":"1 and 2 only","B":"2 and 3 only","C":"1 and 4 only","D":"1, 2, 3 and 4","ans":"A","category":"Polity & Governance","subcategory":"Tribes","tags":["UPSC","2013"],"expl":"Limbu communities are associated with Sikkim and neighbouring Himalayan areas.\nDongaria Kondh are associated with Odisha.\nToda are associated particularly with the Nilgiri Hills of Tamil Nadu.\nBodo communities are primarily associated with Assam.","revisionPoints":"Limbu → Sikkim region.\nDongaria Kondh → Odisha.\nToda → Nilgiri Hills, Tamil Nadu.\nBodo → Assam.\nTribal geography is an important Indian mapping topic."},
{"no":74,"text":"Which of the following represents decreasing liquidity?","A":"Time deposits → Savings deposits → Demand deposits → Currency","B":"Savings deposits → Currency → Time deposits → Demand deposits","C":"Demand deposits → Currency → Savings deposits → Time deposits","D":"Currency → Demand deposits → Savings deposits → Time deposits","ans":"D","category":"Economy","subcategory":"Banking & Monetary Policy","tags":["UPSC","2013"],"expl":"Liquidity refers to how quickly an asset can be used for making payments.\nCurrency has the highest liquidity.\nDemand deposits can generally be used immediately for transactions.\nTime deposits have lower liquidity because withdrawal is subject to conditions or maturity.","revisionPoints":"Liquidity → ease with which an asset can be converted into a means of payment.\nCurrency → highly liquid.\nDemand deposits → highly liquid bank deposits.\nTime deposits → comparatively less liquid."},
{"no":75,"text":"Open Market Operations refer to:","A":"Buying and selling of foreign currencies by commercial banks","B":"Buying and selling of shares by investors","C":"Purchase and sale of government securities by RBI","D":"Purchase and sale of gold by households","ans":"C","category":"Economy","subcategory":"Banking & Monetary Policy","tags":["UPSC","2013"],"expl":"Open Market Operations are conducted by the central bank.\nRBI buys or sells government securities in the market.\nPurchases can inject liquidity into the banking system.\nSales can absorb liquidity from the system.","revisionPoints":"OMO → monetary policy instrument.\nRBI purchases/sells government securities.\nRBI purchase → generally increases system liquidity.\nRBI sale → generally decreases system liquidity."},
{"no":76,"text":"Priority sector lending includes:\n1. Agriculture\n2. Micro and small enterprises\n3. Weaker sections","A":"Agriculture only","B":"Agriculture and micro enterprises only","C":"Micro and small enterprises only","D":"All of the above","ans":"D","category":"Economy","subcategory":"Banking & Monetary Policy","tags":["UPSC","2013"],"expl":"Priority sector lending directs institutional credit toward sectors considered important for inclusive economic development.\nAgriculture is an important priority sector.\nMicro and small enterprises are also included.\nWeaker sections receive priority under the framework.","revisionPoints":"Priority Sector Lending (PSL) → directed bank credit to specified sectors.\nAgriculture is an important PSL category.\nMicro and small enterprises are included.\nWeaker sections are specifically recognised within PSL."},
{"no":77,"text":"Which industry is the largest consumer of water among the following in India?","A":"Textile industry","B":"Paper industry","C":"Sugar industry","D":"Thermal power industry","ans":"D","category":"Economy","subcategory":"Industry","tags":["UPSC","2013"],"expl":"Thermal power generation requires substantial quantities of water.\nWater is used particularly for cooling and related processes.\nLarge thermal power plants therefore have significant water requirements.\nWater availability is an important factor in locating and operating such plants.","revisionPoints":"Thermal power plants require water mainly for cooling.\nWater availability can influence industrial location.\nIndustrial water use varies according to technology and process.\nWater conservation and efficient cooling systems are increasingly important."},
{"no":78,"text":"Which of the following is essential for India to realise the benefits of demographic dividend?","A":"Skill development","B":"Reduction in working-age population","C":"Increase in dependency ratio","D":"Reduction in labour-force participation","ans":"A","category":"Economy","subcategory":"Human Development","tags":["UPSC","2013"],"expl":"A large working-age population alone does not automatically generate economic growth.\nWorkers need appropriate education, skills and employment opportunities.\nSkill development can improve productivity and employability.\nThe demographic dividend depends on effectively utilising the working-age population.","revisionPoints":"Demographic dividend → economic potential arising from a large working-age population.\nRequires education, skills and employment opportunities.\nLower dependency ratio can create favourable demographic conditions.\nHuman capital development is essential for converting demographic potential into economic gains."},
{"no":79,"text":"The Tribhanga pose in Indian classical dance is characterised by:","A":"One leg bent with the body curved in opposite directions at the waist and neck","B":"Standing completely straight without bending","C":"Both legs crossed with the body facing backward","D":"Sitting posture with both knees touching the ground","ans":"A","category":"Art & Culture","subcategory":"Dance","tags":["UPSC","2013"],"expl":"Tribhanga literally refers to a three-bend posture.\nThe body is curved at three points, traditionally involving the head, torso and legs.\nIt is especially associated with Indian sculptural and dance traditions.\nThe pose creates a characteristic graceful, curved posture.","revisionPoints":"Tribhanga → three-bend posture.\nStrongly associated with Indian classical dance and sculpture.\nParticularly prominent in Odissi.\nIt creates an asymmetrical and graceful body position."},
{"no":80,"text":"Consider the following statements about Annie Besant:\n1. She was associated with the Home Rule Movement in India.\n2. She was the founder of the Theosophical Society.\n3. She served as President of the Indian National Congress.\nWhich of the statements given above is/are correct?","A":"1 only","B":"2 only","C":"1 and 3 only","D":"1, 2 and 3","ans":"C","category":"History","subcategory":"Freedom Movement","tags":["UPSC","2013"],"expl":"Annie Besant played an important role in India's Home Rule Movement.\nShe served as President of the Indian National Congress in 1917.\nShe was not the founder of the Theosophical Society; she later became one of its prominent leaders.\nTherefore, statements 1 and 3 are correct.","revisionPoints":"Annie Besant → prominent Theosophist and Indian nationalist.\nAssociated with the Home Rule Movement.\nPresident of the Indian National Congress in 1917.\nThe Theosophical Society was founded before her association with it."},
{"no":81,"text":"The Ilbert Bill controversy was related to:","A":"Introduction of separate electorates","B":"Reduction of land revenue","C":"Removal of restrictions on Indian magistrates trying European offenders","D":"Establishment of local self-government","ans":"C","category":"History","subcategory":"British Administration","tags":["UPSC","2013"],"expl":"The Ilbert Bill was introduced during the tenure of Lord Ripon.\nIt sought to remove racial restrictions in the judicial system.\nThe proposal would allow qualified Indian judges and magistrates to try European British subjects in certain criminal cases.\nStrong opposition from the European community led to modifications of the original proposal.","revisionPoints":"Ilbert Bill → 1883.\nAssociated with Lord Ripon.\nConcerned judicial equality between Indians and Europeans.\nThe controversy exposed racial discrimination in colonial India."},
{"no":82,"text":"A rise in the general price level can be caused by:\n1. Increase in money supply\n2. Decrease in output\n3. Increase in effective demand","A":"1 only","B":"1 and 2 only","C":"2 and 3 only","D":"1, 2 and 3","ans":"D","category":"Economy","subcategory":"Inflation","tags":["UPSC","2013"],"expl":"An increase in money supply can increase spending pressure in the economy.\nA fall in output, with demand remaining strong, can create upward pressure on prices.\nAn increase in effective demand can also cause demand-pull inflation.\nTherefore, all three factors can contribute to a rise in the general price level.","revisionPoints":"Inflation → sustained increase in the general price level.\nIncrease in money supply can create inflationary pressure under certain conditions.\nDecrease in output can create supply-side pressure.\nIncrease in aggregate demand can lead to demand-pull inflation."},
{"no":83,"text":"India's foreign exchange reserves include:\n1. Foreign currency assets\n2. Gold holdings of RBI\n3. Special Drawing Rights","A":"1 only","B":"1, 2 and 3","C":"2 and 3 only","D":"1 and 3 only","ans":"B","category":"Economy","subcategory":"External Sector","tags":["UPSC","2013"],"expl":"Foreign exchange reserves are external assets held by the monetary authority.\nForeign currency assets form an important component.\nRBI's gold holdings are also included.\nSDR holdings with the IMF are another component.","revisionPoints":"Major reserve components include:\nForeign Currency Assets\nGold\nSDRs\nReserve Tranche Position in IMF\nReserves help manage external payment requirements.\nRBI is the custodian and manager of India's foreign exchange reserves."},
{"no":84,"text":"Which of the following is considered the most inflationary method of financing a budget deficit?","A":"Raising taxes","B":"Borrowing from the public","C":"Reducing government expenditure","D":"Creating new money to finance the deficit","ans":"D","category":"Economy","subcategory":"Public Finance","tags":["UPSC","2013"],"expl":"Creating new money directly increases the money supply.\nIf the increase in money supply is not matched by an increase in output, demand pressure can rise.\nThis can generate significant inflationary pressure.\nHence, the source identifies money creation as the most inflationary method.","revisionPoints":"Money creation → direct expansion of money supply.\nInflationary impact depends on the relationship between money supply, output and demand.\nPublic borrowing does not necessarily have the same immediate monetary effect.\nExcessive deficit financing can create macroeconomic instability."},
{"no":85,"text":"If the money supply remains constant but the demand for money increases, what is likely to happen?","A":"Interest rate falls","B":"Interest rate rises","C":"Prices necessarily fall","D":"Money supply increases automatically","ans":"B","category":"Economy","subcategory":"Banking & Monetary Policy","tags":["UPSC","2013"],"expl":"When people demand more money while its supply remains unchanged, there is excess demand for liquidity.\nThe price of borrowing money, represented by the interest rate, tends to rise.\nHigher interest rates can reduce borrowing and investment demand.\nThis reflects the interaction between money demand and money supply.","revisionPoints":"Money market equilibrium depends on money demand and money supply.\nIncrease in money demand with constant supply → upward pressure on interest rates.\nInterest rate is the price of borrowing funds.\nMonetary policy can influence liquidity and interest rates."},
{"no":86,"text":"Cold storage helps extend the shelf life of fruits mainly because:","A":"It increases their water content","B":"It increases photosynthesis","C":"It decreases the rate of respiration","D":"It increases microbial activity","ans":"C","category":"Science & Technology","subcategory":"Biology","tags":["UPSC","2013"],"expl":"Fruits continue to undergo metabolic processes after harvesting.\nRespiration consumes stored food reserves and contributes to ripening and deterioration.\nLower temperatures generally slow down respiration.\nThis helps delay ripening and extend shelf life.","revisionPoints":"Fruits remain metabolically active after harvest.\nLower temperature generally slows respiration.\nCold storage can delay ripening and deterioration.\nProper storage conditions also help control microbial spoilage."},
{"no":87,"text":"Which of the following species are classified as endangered according to the source?\n1. Gharial\n2. Leatherback turtle\n3. Swamp deer","A":"1 only","B":"1 and 2 only","C":"1, 2 and 3","D":"2 and 3 only","ans":"C","category":"Environment & Ecology","subcategory":"Biodiversity","tags":["UPSC","2013"],"expl":"The source places all three species under the endangered category.\nGharial is a specialised crocodilian associated with river ecosystems.\nLeatherback turtle is a marine turtle with a wide oceanic distribution.\nSwamp deer is associated with wetland and grassland habitats.","revisionPoints":"Gharial → specialised riverine crocodilian.\nLeatherback turtle → largest living sea turtle.\nSwamp deer (Barasingha) → important Indian cervid.\nConservation status can vary depending on the classification system and assessment date."},
{"no":88,"text":"Ball bearings are used in machines primarily because they:","A":"Increase friction between surfaces","B":"Increase the weight of the machine","C":"Reduce the effective area of contact between the wheel and axle","D":"Increase the temperature of moving parts","ans":"C","category":"Science & Technology","subcategory":"Physics","tags":["UPSC","2013"],"expl":"Ball bearings consist of small spherical balls placed between moving surfaces.\nThey allow rolling motion instead of direct sliding contact.\nThis significantly reduces frictional resistance in rotating machinery.\nConsequently, mechanical energy losses are reduced.","revisionPoints":"Ball bearings use rolling motion.\nRolling friction is generally lower than sliding friction.\nThey reduce friction in wheels, axles and rotating machinery.\nReduced friction improves mechanical efficiency."},
{"no":89,"text":"Which of the following are examples of optical illusions/phenomena involving apparent visual changes?\n1. Apparent size of the Sun at dusk\n2. Colour of the Sun at dawn\n3. Twinkling of stars","A":"1 only","B":"1 and 2 only","C":"1, 2 and 3","D":"2 and 3 only","ans":"C","category":"Science & Technology","subcategory":"Physics","tags":["UPSC","2013"],"expl":"The apparent size of the Sun near the horizon can be influenced by visual perception and atmospheric effects.\nThe Sun can appear reddish or orange near sunrise and sunset because sunlight travels through a greater atmospheric path.\nStars appear to twinkle because their light is refracted irregularly by moving layers of Earth's atmosphere.\nThe source identifies all three as correct.","revisionPoints":"Atmospheric refraction affects the apparent position and appearance of celestial objects.\nSunrise and sunset colours are strongly related to scattering of light.\nTwinkling of stars results from atmospheric turbulence and refraction.\nThe Sun's apparent size can also involve visual perception."},
{"no":90,"text":"A rainbow is produced mainly due to:\n1. Dispersion of light\n2. Refraction of light\n3. Internal reflection of light","A":"1 only","B":"1 and 2 only","C":"2 and 3 only","D":"1, 2 and 3","ans":"D","category":"Science & Technology","subcategory":"Physics","tags":["UPSC","2013"],"expl":"Sunlight enters raindrops and undergoes refraction.\nDifferent wavelengths are separated through dispersion.\nLight is internally reflected inside the raindrop.\nThe light undergoes refraction again as it exits, producing the visible spectrum.","revisionPoints":"Rainbow formation involves refraction + dispersion + internal reflection.\nWhite sunlight consists of different wavelengths.\nDispersion separates these wavelengths into different colours.\nA primary rainbow involves one internal reflection."},
{"no":91,"text":"Why do transplanted seedlings sometimes fail to survive?","A":"Leaves absorb too much water","B":"Photosynthesis completely stops","C":"Most root hairs are lost during transplantation","D":"Soil becomes permanently infertile","ans":"C","category":"Science & Technology","subcategory":"Botany","tags":["UPSC","2013"],"expl":"Root hairs greatly increase the surface area available for absorption.\nDuring transplantation, delicate root hairs can be damaged or lost.\nThis reduces the plant's ability to absorb water and minerals immediately after transplantation.\nThe plant may therefore experience temporary water stress.","revisionPoints":"Root hairs → specialised structures for absorption.\nThey increase the effective surface area of roots.\nWater and mineral absorption mainly occurs through the root system.\nCareful transplantation helps reduce root damage."},
{"no":92,"text":"Which of the following necessarily contributes to economic growth?","A":"Increase in population alone","B":"Increase in prices","C":"Capital formation in the country","D":"Increase in imports alone","ans":"C","category":"Economy","subcategory":"Basic Concepts","tags":["UPSC","2013"],"expl":"Capital formation involves increasing the stock of productive assets.\nInvestment in machinery, infrastructure and productive facilities can increase productive capacity.\nHigher productive capacity supports expansion of output over time.\nThe source identifies capital formation as the necessary factor among the given choices.","revisionPoints":"Capital formation → addition to the productive capital stock.\nIncludes investment in machines, infrastructure and productive assets.\nEconomic growth refers to sustained increase in real output/income.\nHuman capital and technological progress also contribute to long-term growth."},
{"no":93,"text":"Consider the following statements about viruses:\n1. Viruses lack enzymes necessary for independent energy generation.\n2. Viruses cannot be cultured in any synthetic medium.\n3. Viruses are transmitted only by biological vectors.\nWhich of the statements given above is/are correct?","A":"1 only","B":"1 and 2 only","C":"2 and 3 only","D":"1, 2 and 3","ans":"A","category":"Science & Technology","subcategory":"Biology","tags":["UPSC","2013"],"expl":"Viruses do not possess the complete cellular machinery needed for independent metabolism and energy generation.\nThey depend on host cells for replication.\nThe source considers the second statement incorrect because viruses require living host cells for cultivation rather than ordinary synthetic media.\nThe third statement is also incorrect because viruses can be transmitted through several routes, not only biological vectors.","revisionPoints":"Viruses are acellular infectious agents.\nThey require a host cell for replication.\nThey do not have independent cellular metabolism like living cells.\nTransmission can occur through air, fluids, food, vectors and other routes, depending on the virus."},
{"no":94,"text":"Which of the following are adaptations of desert plants?\n1. Reduced leaves or spines\n2. Thick cuticle\n3. Extensive root systems","A":"1 only","B":"1 and 2 only","C":"2 and 3 only","D":"1, 2 and 3","ans":"D","category":"Science & Technology","subcategory":"Botany","tags":["UPSC","2013"],"expl":"Desert plants face severe water scarcity.\nLeaves may be reduced to spines to decrease transpiration.\nA thick waxy cuticle helps reduce water loss.\nExtensive root systems help plants obtain water from the soil.","revisionPoints":"Desert plants are called xerophytes.\nAdaptations aim to reduce water loss and improve water absorption.\nCommon adaptations → spines, thick cuticle and specialised roots.\nSome plants store water in succulent stems or leaves."},
{"no":95,"text":"Which of the following statements about the four fundamental forces is incorrect?","A":"Gravity is the strongest fundamental force","B":"Electromagnetic force acts between charged particles","C":"Strong nuclear force acts within atomic nuclei","D":"Weak nuclear force is involved in certain radioactive processes","ans":"A","category":"Science & Technology","subcategory":"Physics","tags":["UPSC","2013"],"expl":"Gravity is actually the weakest of the four fundamental interactions at the particle scale.\nThe strong nuclear force is the strongest fundamental interaction.\nElectromagnetic force acts between electrically charged particles.\nThe weak interaction is involved in processes such as beta decay.","revisionPoints":"Four fundamental forces:\nStrong nuclear force\nElectromagnetic force\nWeak nuclear force\nGravitational force\nStrong force → strongest.\nGravity → weakest at the particle scale.\nElectromagnetic force has unlimited range."},
{"no":96,"text":"Why is the discovery of the Higgs boson important?","A":"It helps explain why elementary particles have mass","B":"It proves that gravity does not exist","C":"It explains the formation of all galaxies","D":"It replaces the theory of relativity","ans":"A","category":"Science & Technology","subcategory":"Physics","tags":["UPSC","2013"],"expl":"The Higgs boson is associated with the Higgs field.\nInteraction with the Higgs field gives mass to elementary particles in the Standard Model framework.\nIts discovery provided experimental evidence supporting this mechanism.\nIt is therefore an important component of modern particle physics.","revisionPoints":"Higgs boson → associated with the Higgs field.\nHiggs field mechanism explains the origin of mass for certain elementary particles.\nDiscovered experimentally at CERN's Large Hadron Collider in 2012.\nIt forms part of the Standard Model of particle physics."},
{"no":97,"text":"Mycorrhiza helps plants by:\n1. Increasing the absorptive area of roots\n2. Helping plants tolerate drought and some soil stresses\n3. Providing protection against certain pathogens","A":"1 only","B":"1 and 2 only","C":"2 and 3 only","D":"1, 2 and 3","ans":"D","category":"Science & Technology","subcategory":"Botany","tags":["UPSC","2013"],"expl":"Mycorrhiza is a symbiotic association between fungi and plant roots.\nFungal hyphae extend beyond the root surface and increase the effective absorptive area.\nThis can improve uptake of water and nutrients.\nMycorrhizal associations can also improve tolerance to environmental stresses and provide protection against some pathogens.","revisionPoints":"Mycorrhiza → symbiotic association between fungi and plant roots.\nImproves uptake of phosphorus and other nutrients.\nCan increase tolerance to drought and soil stresses.\nMay help protect roots against certain pathogens."},
{"no":98,"text":"The National Development Council consisted of:\n1. Prime Minister\n2. Union Cabinet Ministers\n3. Chief Ministers of States","A":"1 only","B":"1, 2 and 3","C":"1 and 3 only","D":"2 and 3 only","ans":"B","category":"Economy","subcategory":"Planning","tags":["UPSC","2013"],"expl":"The National Development Council was an important institution associated with India's planning process.\nIt brought together the Union leadership and State governments.\nThe Prime Minister headed the Council.\nChief Ministers participated, providing a forum for Centre-State discussion on development planning.","revisionPoints":"NDC → National Development Council.\nAssociated with India's Five-Year Plan and development planning process.\nIncluded the Prime Minister, Union Ministers and Chief Ministers.\nIt provided a forum for Centre-State coordination."},
{"no":99,"text":"National income is generally expressed as:","A":"Total value of all intermediate goods","B":"Total value of all raw materials","C":"Total money supply in the economy","D":"Money value of final goods and services produced","ans":"D","category":"Economy","subcategory":"National Income","tags":["UPSC","2013"],"expl":"National income measures the economic value generated by an economy during a specified period.\nOnly final goods and services are counted to avoid double counting.\nIntermediate goods are excluded from the final calculation when their value is already incorporated into final goods.\nIt provides an important measure of economic activity.","revisionPoints":"National income relates to the income/output of an economy.\nFocuses on the value of final goods and services.\nExcluding intermediate goods helps avoid double counting.\nCommon national accounting measures include GDP, GNP, NNP and national income."},
{"no":100,"text":"Which institutions provide direct credit assistance to rural households?","A":"SEBI and NABARD","B":"RBI and SEBI","C":"Regional Rural Banks and Land Development Banks","D":"Stock exchanges and commercial companies","ans":"C","category":"Economy","subcategory":"Agriculture & Allied","tags":["UPSC","2013"],"expl":"Regional Rural Banks were established to improve institutional credit access in rural areas.\nLand Development Banks traditionally provide longer-term credit, particularly for agricultural and rural development purposes.\nBoth institutions are associated with rural credit delivery.\nThe source identifies option (c) as the answer.","revisionPoints":"RRBs → focus on rural and agricultural credit.\nRRBs were established to strengthen institutional lending in rural areas.\nLand Development Banks → traditionally associated with long-term agricultural credit.\nRural credit institutions support farmers and rural households."},
];
